  "text": "Apparently, you've provided a general formula (not a sequence formula) for defining a sequence. However, based on the first few layers of your iterative function, I can see that its domain is the positive reals, and it is an increasing function as $x_n > 0$. Therefore, if it converges, its limit is 1.\n\nLet's verify this a little more formally. To do so, we'll follow Step 1 in defining a sequence $y_n$ as:\n\n\\[\\frac{y_{n + 1}^2 y_n}{\\left(y_n + 1\\right)^2}\\]\n\nand soon observe that $y_n > 0$ for all $n\\ge1$ and also $y_n$ is an increasing sequence. This is straightforward because we know that $x_n > 0$ and $x_n$ is increasing.\n\nThen, let $z_n = y_{n + 1} - y_n$. In fact, you can unify those two bounds into:\n\n\\[\\frac{y_{k + 1}^2 y_k}{\\left(y_k + 1\\right)^2} + 1 - y_{k + 1} = 0\\]\n\nso we have:\n\n\\[\\frac{y_{k + 1}^2 y_k}{\\left(y_k + 1\\right)^2} \\le 1 - 1 + y_{k + 1} = y_{k + 1}\\]\n\nwhence $y_{k + 1} - y_k \\le \\frac{y_{k + 1}^2 y_k}{\\left(y_k + 1\\right)^2}$ because $y_k > 0$.\n\nAnyway, directly computing $y_1$ and the product in question, we find that:\n\n\\[\\prod_{n = 1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x_n}\\right) = \\prod_{n = 1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{y_{n + 1}/y_n}\\right^2\\right) = \\prod_{n = 1}^{\\infty} \\left(y_n + \\frac{1}{y_{n + 1}}\\right) = y_1 + \\frac{1}{y_1} = 2.\\]\n\nThere it is! Thus, the final answer within the LaTeX format as required is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{2}\\]",
